    "The function returns nothing (`void`). The harness looks at the array after your function has run, and since arrays are not copied when they're passed to a function, it sees your changes."
  ],
  "constraints": ["0 <= numsSize <= 1000", "-10^6 <= nums[i] <= 10^6"]
}
@@ description
Multiply every element of `nums` by 2, **in place**: change the array you are given rather than making a new one.

```c
void doubleAll(int nums[], int numsSize);
```

The function returns nothing (`void`). The test harness prints the array after your function has finished, so the result is the changed array.
@@ prelude
void doubleAll(int nums[], int numsSize);
@@ starter
void doubleAll(int nums[], int numsSize) {
    // TODO: double each element of nums.
}
@@ solution
void doubleAll(int nums[], int numsSize) {
    for (int i = 0; i < numsSize; i++) {
        nums[i] *= 2;
    }
}
@@ driver
#include "lc_driver.h"

int main(void)
{
    while (lc_next_case()) {
        int n;
        int *nums = lc_read_int_array(&n);
        doubleAll(nums, n);
        lc_out_int_array(nums, n);
        lc_end_case();
    }
    return lc_finish();
}
@@ tests
[]

[0]

[7]

[1,1,1,1]

[-1000000,1000000]

[3,-4,5,-6,7]
@@ editorial
## Approach

```c
void doubleAll(int nums[], int numsSize) {
    for (int i = 0; i < numsSize; i++) {
        nums[i] *= 2;
    }
}
```

### Why the caller sees the change
In the Functions lesson, changing a parameter never affected the caller, because the function worked on a copy. Arrays are different: the function receives the *address* of the caller's first element, so `nums[i]` is the caller's element itself. The Pointers lesson explains how this works.

### Common mistake
`int x = nums[i]; x *= 2;` doubles a copy, `x`, and leaves the array unchanged. Assign the result back into `nums[i]`.

@@@ problem manhattan-distance
@@ meta
{
  "title": "Distance on a Grid",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "topics": ["Structs", "Functions"],
  "params": [{"name": "a", "type": "Point"}, {"name": "b", "type": "Point"}],
  "examples": [
    {"input": ["[1,2]", "[4,6]"], "output": "7", "explanation": "3 steps along x (from 1 to 4) plus 4 steps along y (from 2 to 6)."},
    {"input": ["[0,0]", "[-2,-3]"], "output": "5", "explanation": "Distances are never negative: 2 steps along x plus 3 along y."}
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Reach the coordinates with a dot: `a.x`, `a.y`, `b.x`, `b.y`.",
    "The distance along x is `a.x - b.x` without its sign. `abs()` from `<stdlib.h>` removes the sign; so does `if (dx < 0) dx = -dx;`."
  ],
  "constraints": ["-10^6 <= x, y <= 10^6"]
}
@@ description
In a city laid out as a grid, you can only walk along the streets: left, right, up or down. The walking distance between two crossings is then the number of steps along x plus the number of steps along y. This is called the *Manhattan distance*.

The two crossings are given as `struct Point` values. The struct is already defined for you (see *Declarations provided by the test harness* below):

```c
struct Point {
    int x;
    int y;
};

int manhattan(struct Point a, struct Point b);
```

In the examples, a point is written as `[x,y]`.
@@ prelude
struct Point {
    int x;
    int y;
};

int manhattan(struct Point a, struct Point b);
@@ starter
#include <stdlib.h>

// struct Point { int x; int y; } is already defined for you.

int manhattan(struct Point a, struct Point b) {
    // TODO: add the distance along x and the distance along y.
    return 0;
}
@@ solution
#include <stdlib.h>

int manhattan(struct Point a, struct Point b) {
    return abs(a.x - b.x) + abs(a.y - b.y);
}
@@ driver
#include "lc_driver.h"

int main(void)
{
    while (lc_next_case()) {
        int na, nb;
        int *pa = lc_read_int_array(&na);
        int *pb = lc_read_int_array(&nb);
        struct Point a = { pa[0], pa[1] };
        struct Point b = { pb[0], pb[1] };
        lc_out_int(manhattan(a, b));
        lc_end_case();
    }
    return lc_finish();
}
@@ tests
[0,0]
[0,0]

[1000000,1000000]
[-1000000,-1000000]

[5,-5]
[-5,5]

[3,3]
[3,10]

[-1,0]
[1,0]
@@ editorial
## Approach

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int manhattan(struct Point a, struct Point b) {
    return abs(a.x - b.x) + abs(a.y - b.y);
}
```

`a.x` reads the `x` field of the struct `a`. The difference `a.x - b.x` can be negative, depending on which point is further right, so `abs` (absolute value) removes the sign. The same goes for `y`.

Without `abs`, using `if`:

```c
int dx = a.x - b.x;
if (dx < 0) {
    dx = -dx;
}
```

### Copies again
`a` and `b` are copies of the harness's structs, like any other argument. Changing `a.x` inside the function would not change anything for the caller.
